justment": -0.38, "key_factors": ["R+8 district with an open seat after Buddy Carter's retirement", "Kingston's dominant money edge: ~$1.9M raised plus ~$1.9M outside support vs. Hollowell's ~$163K", "All ten expert raters favor Republicans, a majority calling it Solid or Safe R", "No public polls; prediction markets price roughly 90% Republican"], "rationale": "An eight-point Republican lean, a lopsided fundraising and outside-spending gap for Kingston, and unanimous Republican-favorable expert ratings outweigh a Democratic-friendly national environment in this open coastal Georgia seat. Hollowell's endorsements and affordability messaging keep some upside, but her thin war chest and the district's lean make Kingston the clear favorite.", "watch": "A stronger-than-expected Democratic wave, or late polling showing Hollowell competitive despite her money disadvantage, would tighten this race considerably.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Buddy Carter (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Buddy Carter's retirement leaves this Republican-leaning coastal Georgia seat open, and the fundamentals still favor the GOP. The district carries an eight-point Republican lean, and Jim Kingston enters the fall with a commanding financial advantage — nearly $2 million raised plus about $1.9 million in outside support, against Amanda Hollowell's roughly $163,000 and little remaining cash. Governor Brian Kemp has campaigned for Kingston in Savannah, while Hollowell has drawn a Pete Buttigieg endorsement and focused on affordability issues like gas prices and data centers.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on ratings, spending, and fundamentals. Raters across the spectrum place the seat in Republican hands, most calling it solid or safe, though several say likely Republican. A favorable national climate for Democrats and the open-seat dynamic